# Partial Evaluation

A complete evaluation runs a program from start to finish and answers with a
value. Partial evaluation relaxes that: the program is evaluated as far as the
current environment allows, and when evaluation reaches a call the environment
cannot serve, it stops there. What comes back is the completed part plus a
[residual program][partial-evaluation] — the slice of the program that still
needs a different environment to run. In code mobility terms this is deferred
execution: the residual program is mobile code, ready to be shipped to a node
that has the missing implementations. The [code on demand][code-on-demand]
chapter is the transport half of that story; this chapter is the evaluation
half.

## What the current API does

The schema declares which names a program may reference; the environment
provides their implementations. When a name is in the schema but missing from
the environment, evaluation does not stop gracefully. The reference resolves to
`undefined` and the failure surfaces later, from inside the call, as a
`TypeError`:

```js|json node --input-type=module
import Parse from 'sendscript/parse.ts'
import Stringify from 'sendscript/stringify.ts'
import references from 'sendscript/references.ts'

const { add, remote } = references(['add', 'remote'])
const program = Stringify()(remote(add(1, 2)))

try {
  Parse(['add', 'remote'], { add: (a, b) => a + b })(program)
} catch (error) {
  console.log(`${error.name}: ${error.message}`)
}
```

That is the behavior this chapter builds on: evaluation genuinely does reach the
unresolvable call — everything before it computes — but without help the stop is
a crash, and the computed part is lost.

## Frontier evaluation with a Proxy environment

Fixing the stop needs no change to `Parse`. Wrap the environment in a
[Proxy][proxy]: names the environment serves pass through untouched, names it
does not serve become a frontier stub. `Parse` evaluates the program normally —
computed values compute — and the first unimplemented call stops evaluation with
its fully evaluated arguments in hand:

```js mkdir -p ./example/partial && cat - > ./example/partial/frontier-env.ts
export class MissingImplementation extends Error {
  path: string[]
  args: unknown[]

  constructor (path: string[], args: unknown[]) {
    super(`missing implementation: ${path.join('.')}`)

    this.path = path
    this.args = args
  }
}

export const frontierEnv = (
  env: Record<string, unknown>
): Record<string, unknown> =>
  new Proxy(env, {
    get (target, prop) {
      if (typeof prop !== 'string' || prop in target) return target[prop]

      return (...args: unknown[]) => {
        throw new MissingImplementation([prop], args)
      }
    }
  })
```

Residualizing is serialization in reverse: rebuild the frontier call with
`references` and `Stringify`, and the leftover becomes a program again — the
same shape the code on demand chapter answers with:

```js mkdir -p ./example/partial && cat - > ./example/partial/frontier.ts
import Stringify from 'sendscript/stringify.ts'
import Parse from 'sendscript/parse.ts'
import references from 'sendscript/references.ts'
import { frontierEnv, MissingImplementation } from './frontier-env.ts'

const { add, remote } = references(['add', 'remote'])
const local = { add: (a: number, b: number) => a + b }

const program = Stringify()(remote(add(1, 2)))

try {
  Parse(['add', 'remote'], frontierEnv(local))(program)
} catch (error) {
  if (!(error instanceof MissingImplementation)) throw error

  console.log('stopped at:', error.path.join('.'), 'args:', JSON.stringify(error.args))

  const residual = Stringify()(remote(...(error.args as number[])))

  console.log('residual program:', residual)

  const remoteHost = Parse(['remote'], { remote: (x: number) => x * 10 })

  console.log('remote completion:', remoteHost(residual))
}
```

```bash bash
node ./example/partial/frontier.ts
```

Two things to note. The upstream `add(1, 2)` was computed by the local
environment and its result traveled into the frontier as an evaluated argument —
write programs so partial results flow toward the frontier call. And evaluation
is not transactional: side effects of sibling branches still occur before the
stop, and results that do not flow into the frontier are dropped.

## Staged fragments

The second way keeps the staging explicit. A program is data already, so the
author can hand a whole fragment to the evaluator as a value — a plain array —
and an environment function can evaluate the fragments one at a time. Each
fragment gets its own frontier environment; a fragment whose frontier is hit
hands back a residual instead of a value:

```js mkdir -p ./example/partial && cat - > ./example/partial/staged.ts
import Stringify from 'sendscript/stringify.ts'
import Parse from 'sendscript/parse.ts'
import references from 'sendscript/references.ts'
import { frontierEnv, MissingImplementation } from './frontier-env.ts'

const { add, remote, stage: stageRef } = references(['add', 'remote', 'stage'])
const local = { add: (a: number, b: number) => a + b }

const stage = (fragment: unknown): unknown => {
  const fragmentParse = Parse(['add', 'remote'], frontierEnv(local))

  try {
    return { status: 'done', value: fragmentParse(JSON.stringify(fragment)) }
  } catch (error) {
    if (!(error instanceof MissingImplementation)) throw error

    const path = error.path as ('add' | 'remote')[]
    const residual = Stringify()(references(path)[path[0]](...(error.args as number[])))

    return { status: 'residual', program: residual }
  }
}

const parse = Parse(['stage'], { stage })
const stringify = Stringify()

const result = parse(stringify([
  stageRef(JSON.parse(stringify(add(1, 2)))),
  stageRef(JSON.parse(stringify(remote(add(1, 2)))))
]))

console.log('staged:', JSON.stringify(result))

const residual = (result as unknown as { program: string }[])[1].program

const remoteHost = Parse(['remote'], { remote: (x: number) => x * 10 })

console.log('remote completion:', remoteHost(residual))
```

```bash bash
node ./example/partial/staged.ts
```

Here the outer program is a plain list of two staged calls; the same program
language carries fragments as data. Fragments are written with the ordinary
reflection stubs and carried as values by `JSON.parse`-ing their serialized form
— programs stay ordinary JavaScript right up to the point they become fragment
data.

## Options for first-class support

Both examples above compose exported functions only — `Parse`, `references`,
`Stringify` — and work with the shipped, unmodified API. Making partial
evaluation a supported feature instead of a pattern needs a decision about where
it lives. Three options:

### Option A — a partial mode on Parse

```ts
const parse = Parse(schema, env, { partial: true })
const result = parse(program)
// { status: 'complete', value } |
// { status: 'partial', pending: MissingImplementation-like nodes }
```

The evaluator learns to resolve a schema-declared but unimplemented name into a
pending callee instead of `undefined`. A call over a pending callee does not
invoke anything; its captured path and evaluated arguments become a pending node
in the result, and any computation consuming a pending node becomes pending too
— the residual taints upward. User API changes: one option flag and a structured
result, with strict behavior preserved by default. Format changes: none; the
residual never crosses the wire unless you serialize it.

### Option B — an exported frontier scaffold

```ts
const { parse } = PartialEval(schema, env)
// parse(program) -> { status, value | residual }
```

Promote the Proxy frontier from userland example to an exported helper built on
the same trick. The evaluator is untouched; the package gains one export and a
documented result shape, and gets to own the subtleties (nested namespace
froniers, promise rejections reaching the frontier, residual rebuilding) in a
tested place instead of every user's README copy-paste.

### Option C — a residual node in the program format

Give SENDSCRIPT a native node kind — a cell holding a program plus its missing
names — so a partial evaluation can _return a program as its result value_ and
programs can commute while still carrying unrun work. Programs become
distributable processes. This is the most powerful option and the largest
change: the format itself, both serializers, the parser, and the security story
(what a receiving env must declare to run a cell) all need spec work.

### Comparison

| Option | parse.ts  | User API                     | SENDSCRIPT format | What you get                         |
| ------ | --------- | ---------------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| A      | changes   | + option flag, + result type | unchanged         | principled partial evaluator         |
| B      | unchanged | + one export                 | unchanged         | supported frontier pattern           |
| C      | changes   | unchanged programs           | new node kind     | residual programs as language values |

[partial-evaluation]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partial_evaluation
[proxy]:
  https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Proxy
[code-on-demand]: ./CODE-ON-DEMAND.md
